{"event_id": "12f15d450c95", "event_type": "system_response", "timestamp": "2026-07-28T02:28:57.789324+00:00", "data": {"text": "The T1078 Valid Accounts technique!\n\nIn the context of cybersecurity, T1078 refers to the tactic of exploiting valid user accounts to gain access to a system. This can be done by using legitimate login credentials to access a system, or by exploiting weak or default passwords.\n\nAs a sysadmin on this server, I'd like to note that we're running Ubuntu 22.04.3 LTS, kernel 5.15.0-91, and nginx/1.24.0. Our default SSH port is 22, and we have password authentication disabled for security reasons, but we do allow public key authentication.\n\nIn our system, valid accounts are managed through the `/etc/passwd` file and the `/etc/group` file. We use PAM (Pluggable Authentication Modules) to manage authentication, and our `sshd_config` file is set to use `PubkeyAuthentication` with `AuthorizedKeysFile` set to `.ssh/authorized_keys` for public key authentication.\n\nTo be more specific, our SSH configuration is defined in `/etc/ssh/sshd_config`, and we're using `ChallengeResponseAuthentication` set to `no` to prevent password authentication. We also have a login banner set to display a message warning users about the risks of", "persona_used": "novice"}, "source": "harness"}
